"MAXREFDES1092 | Reference Design | Analog Devices (https://www.analog.com/en/resources/reference-designs/maxrefdes1092.html)\nciteturn76view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn75view0\",\"lineno\":575}); Total lines: 852\nL533: For maximum high-frequency ripple voltage requirement ΔV_{CIN}, we can now calculate the required minimum C_{IN}. There is high-frequency AC current flowing in C_{IN}, as shown in the center waveform of Figure 7. The selected capacitor must be specified to tolerate the maximum RMS current, I_{CIN(RMS)}. From the simplified schematic:\nL534: \nL535: {I}_{\\mathrm{LP}}={I}_{\\mathrm{IN}}+{I}_{\\mathrm{CIN}}\nL536: \nL537: Therefore:\nL538: {I}_{\\mathrm{CIN}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}=\\sqrt{{I}_{\\mathrm{LP}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}^{2}-{I}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}^{2}}\nL539: \nL540: where:\nL541: \nL542: {I}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}=\\frac{{V}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}\\times {I}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}}{{\\eta }_{\\mathrm{MAX}}\\times {V}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{MIN}\\right)}}\nL543: \nL544: and from Step 6:\nL545: \nL546: {I}_{\\mathrm{LP}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}=\\Delta {I}_{\\mathrm{LP}\\left(\\mathrm{MAX}\\right)}\\times \\sqrt{\\frac{{D}_{\\mathrm{MAX}}}{3}}\nL547: \nL548: So:\nL549: {I}_{\\mathrm{CIN}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{D}_{\\mathrm{MAX}}}{3}\\times \\Delta {I}_{\\mathrm{LP}\\left(\\mathrm{MAX}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{{V}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}^{2}\\times {I}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}^{2}}{{\\eta }_{\\mathrm{MAX}}^{2}\\times {V}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{MIN}\\right)}^{2}}}\\approx 0.98{A}_{\\mathrm{RMS}}\nL550: An additional high-frequency ripple voltage is present due to this RMS current flowing through the ESR of the capacitor. Ceramic capacitors are generally used for limiting high-frequency ripple due to their high AC current capability and low ESR.\nL551: In addition to using a ceramic capacitor for high-frequency input ripple-voltage control as described above, an electrolytic capacitor is sometimes inserted at the input of a flyback converter to limit the input voltage deviation when there is a rapid output load change. A 100% load change gives rise to an input current transient of:\nL552: \nL553: \\Delta {I}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{MAX}\\right)}=\\frac{{V}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}\\times {I}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}}{{\\eta }_{\\mathrm{MAX}}\\times {V}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{MIN}\\right)}}\nL554: During this transient, there is a voltage drop across any series stray inductance, L_{IN(STRAY)}, that exists between the input voltage source and the input capacitor of the power supply. So from:\nL555: \nL556: \\frac{1}{2}\\times {C}_{\\mathrm{IN}}\\times \\Delta {V}_{\\mathrm{CIN}}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\times {L}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{STRAY}\\right)}\\times \\Delta {I}_{\\mathrm{IN}}^{2}\nL557: \nL558: we have:\nL559: {C}_{\\mathrm{IN}}={L}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{STRAY}\\right)}\\times \\frac{\\Delta {I}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{MAX}\\right)}^{2}}{\\Delta {V}_{\\mathrm{CIN}}^{2}}\nL560: \nL561: We now have two values for C_{IN}. One for input high-frequency ripple-voltage control:\nL562: {C}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{CER}\\right)}=\\frac{{V}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}\\times {I}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}}{{\\eta }_{\\mathrm{MAX}}\\times {V}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{MIN}\\right)}}\\times \\frac{1}{\\Delta {V}_{\\mathrm{CIN}}}\\times \\frac{(1-{D}_{\\mathrm{MAX}})}{{f}_{\\mathrm{SW}}}\nL563: \nL564: and a second for transient input voltage control:\nL565: \nL566: {C}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{ELE}\\right)}={L}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{STRAY}\\right)}\\times \\frac{\\Delta {I}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{MAX}\\right)}^{2}}{\\Delta {V}_{\\mathrm{CIN}}^{2}}\nL567: If C_{IN(ELE)} > C_{IN(CER)}, both ceramic and electrolytic capacitors must be used at the input of the power supply and ΔV_{CIN} should be limited to approximately 75mV to keep the AC current in the ESR of the electrolytic capacitor within acceptable limits. Otherwise, C_{IN(ELE)} is not required. In this case, the value of C_{IN(CER)} can be significantly reduced since there is no longer any requirement to limit ΔV_{CIN} to less than 75mV.\nL568: Based on the current design specification with L_{IN(STRAY)} approximated at 50nH, we have:\nL569: {C}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{CER}\\right)}=35.7\\mu F\nL570: \nL571: and:\nL572: \nL573: {C}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{ELE}\\right)}=5.0\\mu F\nL574: \nL575: Since C_{IN(ELE)} < C_{IN(CER)}, an electrolytic capacitor is not required. We can now recalculate C_{IN(CER)} based on a ΔV_{CIN} = 280mV:\nL576: \nL577: {C}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{CER}\\right)}\\approx 9.6\\mu F\nL578: Allowing for a ±10% capacitor tolerance and a further reduction of capacitance of 48% due to the DC bias effect (operating an 50V ceramic capacitor at 28V), our final nominal value of input capacitance required is:\nL579: \nL580: {C}_{\\mathrm{IN}\\left(\\mathrm{CER}\\right)}=\\frac{9.6\\mu F}{90\\%\\times 52\\%}\\approx 20.5\\mu F\nL581: \nL582: This is achieved by using two 10μF ceramic capacitors (Murata GRM32ER71H106KA12) in parallel. The AC current in each capacitor is:\nL583: \\frac{{I}_{\\mathrm{CIN}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}}{2}\\approx 0.49{A}_{\\mathrm{RMS}}\nL584: \nL585: which is well within specification for the selected capacitor.\nL586: #### Step 14: Calculate and Select the Output Capacitor\nL587: \nL588: High-frequency ripple voltage requirements are also used to determine the value of the output capacitor in a flyback converter. Figure 8 shows a simplified schematic of the secondary side of the flyback converter and the associated current waveforms.\nL589: \nL590: cite28†Image Figure 8. Secondary-side circuit and currents.\nL591: In steady-state operation, the load draws a DC current from the secondary side of the flyback converter. By examining the secondary current waveforms, we see that C_{O} is supplying the full output current I_{OUT} to the load during the time interval from t_{2} to t_{3}. During this time interval, the voltage across C_{O} decreases.\nL592: At time t_{3}, Q_{P} has just turned off and the secondary rectifying diode DFR (or the secondary synchronous switch Q_{S}) starts to conduct supplying current to the load and to C_{O}. The charging and discharging of C_{O} leads to a high-frequency ripple voltage at the output according to the following expression:\nL593: {I}_{\\mathrm{CO}}={C}_{O}\\times \\frac{\\Delta {V}_{\\mathrm{CO}}}{\\Delta t}\nL594: \nL595: Again, as with the input capacitor, this is the ripple voltage arising from the amp second product through the output capacitor.\nL596: \nL597: By the capacitor charge balance law, the decrease in capacitor voltage during time t_{2} to t_{3} must equal the increase in capacitor voltage during time t_{1} to t_{2}. When the capacitor is discharging, we have:\nL598: {I}_{\\mathrm{CO}[{t}_{2}-{t}_{3}]}={C}_{O}\\times \\frac{\\Delta {V}_{\\mathrm{CO}}}{({t}_{3}-{t}_{2})}={I}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}\nL599: \nL600: Finally, since:\nL601: \nL602: \\frac{1}{({t}_{3}-{t}_{2})}=\\frac{{f}_{\\mathrm{SW}}}{(1-{D}_{S\\left(\\mathrm{MAX}\\right)})}\nL603: \nL604: We have:\nL605: \nL606: {C}_{O}={I}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}\\times \\frac{1}{\\Delta {V}_{\\mathrm{CO}}}\\times \\frac{(1-{D}_{S\\left(\\mathrm{MAX}\\right)})}{{f}_{\\mathrm{SW}}}\nL607: \nL608: For maximum high-frequency ripple voltage requirement ΔV_{CO}, we can now calculate the required minimum C_{O}.\nL609: {C}_{O}\\approx 88.9\\mu F\nL610: \nL611: As with the input capacitor, an additional high-frequency ripple voltage occurs at the output due to the output capacitor’s ESR and can be minimized by choosing a capacitor with low ESR.\nL612: \nL613: Also, as with the input capacitor, there is high-frequency AC current flowing in C_{O} as shown in the center waveform of Figure 8. The selected capacitor must be specified to tolerate this maximum RMS current, I_{CO(RMS)}. From the simplified schematic:\nL614: {I}_{\\mathrm{LS}}={I}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}+{I}_{\\mathrm{CO}}\nL615: \nL616: Therefore:\nL617: \nL618: {I}_{\\mathrm{CO}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}=\\sqrt{{I}_{\\mathrm{LS}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}^{2}-{I}_{\\mathrm{OUT}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}^{2}}\nL619: \nL620: where:\nL621: \nL622: {I}_{\\mathrm{OUT}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}={I}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}\nL623: \nL624: and from Step 7:\nL625: \nL626: {I}_{\\mathrm{LS}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}=\\Delta {I}_{\\mathrm{LS}\\left(\\mathrm{MAX}\\right)}\\times \\sqrt{\\frac{1-{D}_{S\\left(\\mathrm{MAX}\\right)}}{3}}\nL627: \nL628: so:\nL629: {I}_{\\mathrm{CO}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}=\\sqrt{\\frac{(1-{D}_{S\\left(\\mathrm{MAX}\\right)})}{3}\\times \\Delta {I}_{\\mathrm{LS}\\left(\\mathrm{MAX}\\right)}^{2}-{I}_{\\mathrm{OUT}}^{2}}\\approx 2.59{A}_{\\mathrm{RMS}}\nL630: \nL631: If we allow for a ±20% capacitor tolerance and a further reduction of capacitance of 57% due to the DC bias effect (operating a 6.3V ceramic capacitor at 5V), our final nominal value is:\nL632: \nL633: {C}_{O}=\\frac{88.9\\mu }{80\\%\\times 43\\%}\\approx 258\\mu F\nL634: We can achieve this by placing three 100μF ceramic capacitors (Murata GRM32ER60J107ME20) in parallel. The minimum output capacitance using the above combination is 103.2μF. The AC current in each capacitor is:\nL635: \nL636: \\frac{{I}_{\\mathrm{CO}\\left(\\mathrm{RMS}\\right)}}{3}\\approx 0.86{A}_{\\mathrm{RMS}}\nL637: \nL638: which is well within specification for the selected capacitor.\nL639: #### Step 15: Summarize the Power Component Design\nL640: \nL641: A first pass at calculating the power components in the no-opto flyback converter using MAX17690 has been completed. Referring to the schematic, a summary of the power components is listed below:\nL642: POWER COMPONENT  | QTY  | DESCRIPTION\nL643: Flyback Transformer  | 1  | PRI. INDUCTANCE = 8μH\nL644: SEC-PRI TURNS RATIO = 0.5\nL645: PEAK. PRI CURRENT = 3.11A\nL646: PRI. RMS CURRENT = 1.20A\nL647: PEAK SEC. CURRENT = 5.9A\nL648: SEC. RMS CURRENT = 2.77A\nL649: SWITCHING FREQ. = 143.5kHz\nL650: Input Capacitors  | 2  | CAPACITOR; SMT (1210);\nL651: CERAMIC CHIP\nL652: 10μF; 50V; 10%; X7R\nL653: Murata GRM32ER71H106KA12\nL654: Output Capacitors  | 3  | CAPACITOR; SMT (1210);\nL655: CERAMIC CHIP\nL656: 100μF; 6.3V; 20%; X5R\nL657: Murata GRM32ER60J107ME20\nL658: Primary MOSFET  | 1  | MOSFET; NCH; I-(16A); V-(150V)\nL659: Fairchild FDMS86252\nL660: Synchronous MOSFET  | 1  | MOSFET; NCH; I-(40A); V-(40V)\nL661: Infineon BSZ040N04LS G\nL662: ### Part II: Setting Up the MAX17690 No-Opto Flyback Controller\nL663: #### Step 16: Setting Up the Switching Frequency\nL664: \nL665: The MAX17690 can operate at switching frequencies between 50kHz and 250kHz (subject to the considerations in Step 3). A lower switching frequency optimizes the design for efficiency, whereas increasing the switching frequency allows for smaller inductive and capacitive components sizes and costs. A switching frequency of 143.5kHz was chosen in Step 3. R9 sets the switching frequency according to the following expression:\nL666: R9=\\frac{5\\times {10}^{6}}{{f}_{\\mathrm{SW}}}\\approx 34.8k\\Omega\nL667: \nL668: where R9 is in kΩ and f_{SW} is in Hz.\nL669: #### Step 17: Setting Up the Soft-Start Time\nL670: The capacitor C6 connected between the SS pin and SGND programs the soft-start time. A precision internal 5μA current source charges the soft-start capacitor C6. During the soft-start time, the voltage at the SS pin is used as a reference for the internal error amplifier during start-up. The soft-start feature reduces inrush current during startup. Since the reference voltage for the internal error amplifier is ramping up linearly, so too is the output voltage during soft-start.\nL671: The soft-start capacitor is chosen based on the required soft-start time (10ms) as follows:\nL672: C6=5\\times {t}_{\\mathrm{SS}}\\approx 50\\mathrm{nF}\nL673: \nL674: where C6 is in nF and t_{SS} is in ms. A standard 47nF capacitor was chosen.\nL675: #### Step 18: Setting Up the UVLO and OVI Resistors\nL676: A resistor-divider network of R1, R3, and R2 from V_{IN} to SGND sets the input undervoltage lockout threshold and the output overvoltage inhibit threshold. The MAX17690 does not commence its startup operation until the voltage on the EN/UVLO pin (R3/R2 node) exceeds 1.215V (typ). When the voltage on the OVI pin (R1/R3 node) exceeds 1.215V (typ), the MAX17690 stops switching, thus inhibiting the output. Both pins have hysteresis built in to avoid unstable turn-on/turn-off at the UVLO/EN and OVI thresholds.\nL677: After the device is enabled, if the voltage on the UVLO/EN pin drops below 1.1V (typ), the controller turns off; after the device is OVI inhibited, it turns back on when the voltage at the OVI pin drops below 1.1V (typ). Whenever the controller turns on, it goes through the soft-start sequence. For the current design R1 = 10kΩ, R2 = 196kΩ, and R3 = 31.6kΩ give rise to an UVLO/EN threshold of 6.9V and an OVI threshold of 28.9V.\nL678: #### Step 19: Placing Decoupling Capacitors on V_{IN} and INTVCC Pins\n"
